Task 2 — Add a Download button to the Home Screen

Current behavior

The Home Screen contains:

* The Alva logo and tagline.
* Play, Watch Mode, Practice Ground, and Discover navigation.
* A top-right utility group containing Help, Controller, and Settings.
* A scrolling credits panel.
* A bottom footer containing the developer attribution.

Help and Controller are currently disabled placeholder buttons. Settings is functional.

Required implementation

Add one new Download button in the bottom-right corner of the Home Screen.

This must be a visible, icon-only button using a download symbol.

It is a placeholder for a future feature and must not perform any action.

Placement

The download button should sit at the far right of the Home Screen’s bottom footer area, visually aligned with the existing layout.

Recommended implementation:

* Add the button inside the existing .home-footer element in js/screens/home-screen.js.
* Keep the developer attribution on the left.
* Place the Download button on the far right.
* Use the existing footer flex layout and appropriate spacing.

This positioning should avoid covering the main menu or the scrolling credits.

Do not place the button inside .home-utility-buttons, since that group belongs in the top-right corner.

Button appearance

The new Download button should:

* Use a compact square design.
* Follow the visual styling of Alva’s existing utility buttons.
* Have a dark, subtle background.
* Use a thin, understated border.
* Display a centered download icon.
* Use the existing theme colors rather than hardcoded unrelated colors.
* Have consistent internal padding and alignment.
* Remain visible without attracting excessive attention.

It should appear as a native part of Alva’s interface, not a separate design element.

Download icon

In js/ui/icons.js, introduce a new shared icon:

ICONS.download

Create a simple inline SVG using the existing svg() helper.

The icon should represent a downward-facing arrow entering a download tray or horizontal base.

Follow the conventions of the other icons:

* Use the existing 24 × 24 SVG coordinate system.
* Inherit currentColor through existing CSS.
* Use simple, clean stroked geometry.
* Keep stroke thickness visually consistent.
* Avoid external images, emoji, icon libraries, or dependencies.

The icon must be recognizable at small sizes.

Non-functional behavior

The Download button must be completely non-functional.

Requirements:

* Use a native HTML button.
* Set type="button".
* Set disabled: true.
* Do not attach a click handler.
* Do not trigger downloads.
* Do not open a URL.
* Do not navigate to another screen.
* Do not display a popup, tooltip indicating an active download, or dialog.
* Do not modify settings or game state.
* Do not add it to keyboard/gamepad menu navigation using data-nav.

Follow the disabled-button pattern already used by the Help and Controller buttons.

The button is intentionally visible but unavailable.

Localization and accessibility

Add a new localized string:

* English: home.download → Download
* French: home.download → Télécharger

Use the existing localization helper to provide an accessible aria-label and, if consistent with nearby controls, a localized title.

The SVG itself must remain decorative and hidden from assistive technology, following the shared icon conventions.

Native disabled semantics should communicate that the feature is currently unavailable.

Responsive requirements

The button must display correctly on:

* Desktop landscape screens.
* Laptop-sized windows.
* Tablet landscape layouts.
* Small mobile landscape layouts.
* Narrow or portrait browser windows.
* Short-height mobile screens.

Respect the existing safe-area variables:

* --safe-r
* --safe-b
* --safe-l

Ensure the button:

* Does not overlap the developer attribution.
* Remains aligned to the right.
* Does not disappear outside the viewport.
* Does not significantly increase the footer height on short screens.
* Does not obstruct menu actions or other interactive elements.
* Does not introduce unwanted horizontal scrolling.

If required, reduce the button’s dimensions at smaller viewport heights using Alva’s existing CSS conventions.

